Questions:
Find Command Operations with Answers and Explanations
Basic Find Command Operations
Find files by name:
1. Find all .txt files in /var/log

bash
find /var/log -name "*.txt"
Explanation: -name matches the base filename against the pattern. Quotes prevent shell expansion.

2. Find files named hosts in the entire system

bash
find / -name hosts 2>/dev/null
Explanation: 2>/dev/null suppresses permission denied errors. Searches from root /.

3. Case-insensitive search for files named passwd

bash
find / -iname passwd 2>/dev/null
Explanation: -iname performs case-insensitive matching.

Find files by type:
4. Find all directories in /home

bash
find /home -type d
Explanation: -type d restricts search to directories only.

5. Find all symbolic links in /etc

bash
find /etc -type l
Explanation: -type l finds symbolic links.

6. Find all regular files in /tmp

bash
find /tmp -type f
Explanation: -type f finds regular files (not directories, links, etc.).

Find files by size:
7. Find files larger than 100MB in /var

bash
find /var -type f -size +100M
Explanation: -size +100M = larger than 100 megabytes. M = 1024×1024 bytes.

8. Find empty files in current directory

bash
find . -type f -empty
Explanation: -empty matches empty files and directories. -type f restricts to files.

9. Find files between 1MB and 10MB

bash
find / -type f -size +1M -size -10M 2>/dev/null
Explanation: Two -size tests combined: >1MB AND <10MB.

Find files by permissions:
10. Find files with SUID bit set

bash
find / -type f -perm /4000 2>/dev/null
# OR
find / -type f -perm -u=s 2>/dev/null
Explanation: -perm /4000 = any SUID bit set. / means "any of these bits". Octal 4000 = SUID.

11. Find world-writable files

bash
find / -type f -perm /002 2>/dev/null
# OR
find / -type f -perm /o=w 2>/dev/null
Explanation: -perm /002 = world-writable bit set. Security concern!

12. Find files owned by specific user

bash
find / -user jdoe 2>/dev/null
Explanation: -user jdoe matches files owned by user jdoe (name or UID).

Find files by time:
13. Find files modified in last 24 hours

bash
find / -type f -mtime 0
Explanation: -mtime 0 = modified within last 24 hours. 0 means "0 days ago".

14. Find files accessed more than 30 days ago

bash
find / -type f -atime +30
Explanation: -atime +30 = last accessed more than 30 days ago. + means "greater than".

15. Find files changed (status) in last 7 days

bash
find / -type f -ctime -7
Explanation: -ctime -7 = status changed within last 7 days. Status changes include permission, ownership changes. - means "less than".

Common RHCSA-Style Scenarios
File management tasks:
16. Find and delete all .tmp files in /tmp

bash
find /tmp -type f -name "*.tmp" -delete
# Safer alternative:
find /tmp -type f -name "*.tmp" -exec rm -f {} \;
Explanation: -delete removes files. Always test first with -print instead of -delete.

17. Find and compress all log files older than 7 days

bash
find /var/log -name "*.log" -mtime +7 -exec gzip {} \;
Explanation: -exec gzip {} \; runs gzip on each file. {} is replaced by filename.

18. Find and change permissions of files owned by a specific user

bash
find /home -user jdoe -type f -exec chmod 644 {} \;
Explanation: Changes permissions to 644 (rw-r--r--) for all jdoe's regular files.

System administration tasks:
19. Find all files modified after a certain date for audit

bash
touch -t 202312010000 /tmp/timestamp
find / -type f -newer /tmp/timestamp 2>/dev/null
Explanation: Creates a reference file with specific timestamp, finds files newer than it.

20. Locate configuration files that have been changed

bash
find /etc -type f -name "*.conf" -newer /etc/passwd
Explanation: Finds .conf files in /etc modified more recently than /etc/passwd.

21. Find core dump files and remove them

bash
find / -type f -name "core" -o -name "core.*" -delete 2>/dev/null
# OR safer:
find / -type f \( -name "core" -o -name "core.*" \) -exec rm -f {} \; 2>/dev/null
Explanation: \( ... \) groups conditions. -o = OR. Finds files named "core" or "core.*".

User/Group related:
22. Find all files owned by user jdoe

bash
find / -user jdoe 2>/dev/null | head -20
Explanation: Lists first 20 files owned by jdoe. Pipe to head to avoid huge output.

23. Find files with group developers

bash
find / -group developers 2>/dev/null
Explanation: -group developers matches group name or GID.

24. Find files where user doesn't have read permission

bash
find /home/jdoe -type f ! -readable
Explanation: ! -readable = not readable by current user. ! negates the test.

Advanced/Practical Exam Problems
Combined criteria searches:
25. Find .conf files modified in last 2 days

bash
find /etc -name "*.conf" -mtime -2
Explanation: Combines name pattern with time condition.

26. Find executable files owned by root in /usr/local/bin

bash
find /usr/local/bin -type f -executable -user root
Explanation: -executable matches files executable by current user.

27. Find PDF files larger than 5MB in user home directories

bash
find /home -type f -name "*.pdf" -size +5M
Explanation: Multiple criteria: type, name pattern, size.

Action execution with find:
28. Find files and list them with ls -l

bash
find /var/log -type f -name "*.log" -exec ls -l {} \;
# OR
find /var/log -type f -name "*.log" -ls
Explanation: Two methods: -exec ls -l or built-in -ls action.

29. Find and copy files to backup directory

bash
find /etc -name "*.conf" -exec cp {} /backup/ \;
# To preserve directory structure:
find /etc -name "*.conf" -exec cp --parents {} /backup/ \;
Explanation: --parents preserves directory structure in target.

30. Find and count lines in text files

bash
find /var/log -name "*.log" -exec wc -l {} \;
# Total lines in all files:
find /var/log -name "*.log" -exec wc -l {} + | tail -1
Explanation: + at end groups files for single wc command. tail -1 shows total.

Permission and security:
31. Find files with 777 permissions

bash
find / -type f -perm 777 2>/dev/null
Explanation: -perm 777 = exactly 777 permissions. Security risk!

32. Find files with no owner (orphaned files)

bash
find / -nouser 2>/dev/null
Explanation: -nouser = user ID doesn't exist in /etc/passwd.

33. Find files with sticky bit set

bash
find / -type d -perm /1000 2>/dev/null
# OR for directories only (sticky bit meaningful on dirs):
find / -type d -perm -1000 2>/dev/null
Explanation: -perm /1000 = sticky bit set. Usually on directories like /tmp.

Key Concepts Explained:
Permission Tests:
-perm 644 = exactly 644

-perm -644 = ALL bits in 644 must be set (at least these permissions)

-perm /644 = ANY bit in 644 is set (matches 644, 755, etc.)

Time Tests:
-mtime n = n*24 hours ago

-mtime +n = more than n days ago

-mtime -n = less than n days ago

-mtime 0 = last 24 hours

Size Suffixes:
c = bytes

k = kilobytes (1024 bytes)

M = megabytes (1024² bytes)

G = gigabytes (1024³ bytes)

Security Best Practices:
Test first: Replace -delete with -print

Use -execdir instead of -exec when possible

Quote patterns: -name "*.txt"

Handle errors: 2>/dev/null for system-wide searches

Be specific: Use full paths, not relative

Common Pitfalls:
bash
# WRONG: Will only print .log files
find . -name "*.txt" -o -name "*.log" -print

# CORRECT: Parentheses group conditions
find . \( -name "*.txt" -o -name "*.log" \) -print

# WRONG: Shell expands pattern
find . -name *.txt

# CORRECT: Pattern quoted
find . -name "*.txt"
Useful Combinations:
bash
# Find and archive
find /home -name "*.doc" -print0 | tar -czf docs.tar.gz --null -T -

# Find and calculate total size
find /var/log -name "*.log" -exec du -ch {} + | grep total$

# Find and process with xargs (safer with -0)
find /tmp -name "*.tmp" -print0 | xargs -0 rm -f
